[07:36] CANDIDATE: SQLite on the laptop agent just stores the command history and the results, with an id per command. The Claude API is used on the agent to turn a natural language instruction into a list of commands. The phone never needs the database; it asks the relay server for the history, and the agent uploads each result when it finishes, so there's one writer per record and no real sync problem.
[08:17] INTERVIEWER: Now, when the phone sends a command but the laptop is asleep or the socket has dropped — what happens to that command? Do you queue it, retry it, or lose it?
    [item=deep_dive; model=deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash first_token=1010ms wall=1.2s; activity=## Activity (what they did while speaking, and since) | While speaking (27 s): | - -2.0–26.9s pointer on “buy-sell transactions as he likes” in the problem (28.9 s) | Since they last spoke (12 s): no typing or drawing.]
<<READY 08:17>>
[08:17] CANDIDATE: Right now it's lost. The phone gets an error if the socket is down, but if the laptop is just asleep the server accepts the command and it never runs. I'd add a per-command queue on the server with a time to live, deliver on reconnect, and use command ids so a retry can't run twice.
[08:49] INTERVIEWER: Good — so that gives you at-least-once delivery down to the agent. How would the agent make sure a redelivered command doesn't execute twice?
    [item=deep_dive; model=deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash first_token=1028ms wall=1.6s; activity=## Activity (what they did while speaking, and since) | While speaking (21 s): | - -2.0–21.2s pointer on “buy-sell transactions as he likes” in the problem (23.2 s) | Since they last spoke (11 s): no typing or drawing.]
<<READY 08:49>>
[08:49] CANDIDATE: The agent would keep the last few hundred command ids it has executed in SQLite, with their results. If a command arrives with an id it already has, it skips execution and just sends the stored result again. The check and the insert would happen in one transaction so a crash in between can't cause a double run.
[09:20] INTERVIEWER: That’s a clean idempotency story. On the fundamentals side — how does a WebSocket upgrade actually happen over HTTP?
    [item=deep_dive; model=deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash first_token=882ms wall=1.1s; activity=## Activity (what they did while speaking, and since) | While speaking (22 s): | - -2.0–21.9s pointer on “buy-sell transactions as he likes” in the problem (23.9 s) | Since they last spoke (8 s): no typing or drawing.]
<<READY 09:20>>
[09:20] CANDIDATE: The client sends a normal HTTP GET with an Upgrade: websocket header, a Connection: Upgrade header, and a random Sec-WebSocket-Key. The server answers 101 Switching Protocols with a Sec-WebSocket-Accept header, which is the key hashed with a fixed GUID and base64 encoded. After that the same TCP connection carries WebSocket frames instead of HTTP.
