    [item=deep_dive; model=deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash first_token=1580ms wall=2.9s; activity=## Activity (what they did while speaking, and since) | While speaking (2 s): | - -2.0–1.9s pointer on “buy-sell transactions as he likes” in the problem (3.9 s) | Since they last spoke (7 s): no typing or drawing.]
<<READY 10:09>>
[10:09] CANDIDATE: Because hash based collections like HashMap first use hashCode to pick a bucket and then equals to compare inside it. If two objects are equal but have different hash codes they land in different buckets, so lookups fail and you can get duplicates in a HashSet.
[10:38] INTERVIEWER: Good, that’s the contract exactly. In SQL — explain inner, left, right and full outer joins, and tell me when you’d reach for a left join.
    [item=deep_dive; model=deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash first_token=897ms wall=1.7s; activity=## Activity (what they did while speaking, and since) | While speaking (17 s): | - -2.0–17.3s pointer on “buy-sell transactions as he likes” in the problem (19.3 s) | Since they last spoke (15 s): no typing or drawing.]
<<READY 10:38>>
[10:38] CANDIDATE: An inner join returns only rows that match in both tables. A left join keeps every row from the left table and fills the right side with nulls where there's no match, right join is the mirror, and full outer keeps everything from both. I'd use a left join for something like listing all users and their orders, including users who have no orders yet.
[11:14] INTERVIEWER: Right, that covers them well. Last one — in React, what's the difference between state and props, and when would you lift state up?
    [item=deep_dive; model=deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash first_token=2275ms wall=2.9s; activity=## Activity (what they did while speaking, and since) | While speaking (25 s): | - -2.0–24.6s pointer on “buy-sell transactions as he likes” in the problem (26.6 s) | Since they last spoke (9 s): no typing or drawing.]
<<READY 11:14>>
[11:14] CANDIDATE: Props are inputs passed from a parent and are read only in the child, state is data a component owns and can change. When two sibling components need the same data, I lift the state up to their closest common parent and pass it down as props along with callbacks to update it.
